# Decision Log

## 2026-08-20

### D1. Separate exact closure from persistent defect

`Phi=0`, nonzero critical defect, symmetry-relative motion, protected
recurrence, and transient repair are different state types. No theorem may move
between them silently.

### D2. Keep first-order phase data

A positive square erases at least a binary sign and, in the nilpotent Hilbert
case, an entire `U(1)` polarization. The first-order charge and shared-line
representation remain source data until a selection theorem removes them.

### D3. Derive reversible and dissipative operators together where possible

The nilpotent single-charge factorization is preferred over independent `K` and
`Gamma` choices. It is a local structural theorem, not yet a physical action.

### D4. Use curved internal differentials away from exact closure

For an inner superconnection `Q`, the adjoint differential satisfies
`d_Q^2=ad_(Q^2)`. Zero, central nonzero, and noncentral curvature must be kept
separate.

### D5. Do not identify Jordan and Lie curvature shadows

The residual Hessian uses the anticommutator/Jordan action of curvature. The
twisted adjoint differential uses its commutator/Lie action. They are
complementary consequences of one multiplication, not identical operators.

### D6. Make admissible variations native source data

The radial no-go proves that nonzero curvature cannot be a critical point of
`1/2||Q^2||^2` on the unrestricted odd charge space. Fixed symbol, topology,
boundary conditions, normalization, or another admissible stratum must be
specified before criticality and Hessians are computed.

### D7. Distinguish ambient and intrinsic Hessians

Projecting an ambient Hessian after differentiation is insufficient when the
ambient gradient is nonzero. The admissible stratum's second fundamental term
must be included. On a Clifford phase circle this term exactly converts a
positive ambient direction into an intrinsic zero mode.

### D8. Locate scalar ambiguity in the center

Commutator data determine curvature only modulo the center of the selected
operator algebra. Central scalar values must come from the Jordan/action/value
layer. Their structural count is the number of symmetry-orbits of simple
blocks, not the matrix dimension.

